<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>A blank die has six faces. Write a whole number from 1 to 9 on each face (numbers may repeat) so that, when the die is rolled,</p><p style="text-align:center;"><m>P(\text{even number}) = \dfrac{{4}}{6}</m> and <m>P(\text{number greater than } {6}) = \dfrac{{3}}{6}</m>.</p><p>Each fraction is out of 6 because the die has six faces.</p><p>Give your six numbers as a list, for example <c>[1,2,2,5,8,9]</c>.</p><div><fillin characters="20" name="ans1"/>  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>Decide how many faces of each kind you need, then choose numbers. You need <m>{4}</m> even faces and <m>{3}</m> faces greater than <m>{6}</m>. A number such as 8 is both even and greater than <m>{6}</m>, so it counts for both.</p><p>One way: <m>{3}</m> faces showing 8 (even and greater than <m>{6}</m>), <m>{1}</m> showing 2 (even, not greater), <m>{0}</m> showing 9 (odd, greater) and <m>{2}</m> showing 1 (odd, not greater). That gives the die <m>{\left[ 1 , 1 , 2 , 8 , 8 , 8 \right]}</m>.</p><p>Any list of six numbers from 1 to 9 with the right counts is accepted. Remember that <q>greater than <m>{6}</m></q> does not include <m>{6}</m> itself.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>\left[ 1 , 1 , 2 , 8 , 8 , 8 \right] </m></p></answer>
</stack-static>